2) Why it is unlikely to contain China
1. It mistakes regional dominance for global power
Preventing Chinese influence in Panama, Venezuela or Brazil does not seriously diminish China’s underlying power. China’s position rests on:
- an enormous industrial base;
- dominance in parts of critical-mineral processing;
- major positions in batteries, solar technology, shipbuilding and manufacturing;
- extensive trade relationships;
- infrastructure finance;
- a growing military and technological base;
- the ability to offer markets and investment without demanding formal alliances.

Latin America matters greatly, but controlling political outcomes there cannot reverse these structural advantages.
(Some numbers: China now accounts for 20.9% of total exports from Lat. Am. and the Caribbean. This puts China ahead of the US (16.4%) and the EU (12.4%). 21 of 33 Lat. Am.countries have officially joined China's Belt and Road Initiative)

2. Tariffs impose costs but have not compelled strategic retreat
The tariff war did harm China, but it did not achieve the larger objective of forcing China to abandon its industrial model or relinquish its position.

China can absorb pain, redirect trade, subsidize strategic sectors and wait for American policy to change. Tariffs can therefore be one instrument of economic security, but indiscriminate tariffs are not a China strategy.

The effective leverage would come from coordination among the United States, EU, Japan, South Korea, Canada and other advanced economies on:
- subsidies and overcapacity;
- market access and public procurement;
- export controls;
- technology transfer;
- investment screening;
- critical minerals;
- WTO reform and enforcement.

Attacking these partners separately allows Beijing to negotiate with each from a stronger position.

3. It exchanges allies for geography
China has few treaty allies in the Western sense. Its advantage is different: it can frequently present itself as economically useful and less openly intrusive in domestic politics - even though it also uses coercion, surveillance, trade restrictions and political influence.

The US possesses something potentially more valuable: a global alliance network that China cannot presently reproduce. Undermining Canada, Europe, South Korea and other allies voluntarily destroys that comparative advantage. Threatening allies does not necessarily make their governments comply. It allows their leaders to defend national dignity against a foreign aggressor. Even citizens dissatisfied with their own government may rally behind it.

The same applies in Latin America more broadly. Memories of American interventions are politically powerful. A policy intended to remove Chinese influence can make China appear the less threatening partner.

Trump’s August 2026 instruction to reduce exercises with South Korea - followed by the cancellation of a planned amphibious exercise - illustrates the problem. It linked alliance cooperation to Seoul’s refusal to support American action against Iran. That teaches allies that security guarantees are conditional on obedience outside the purpose of the alliance.
That does not strengthen American leverage against China. It encourages South Korea and others to hedge between Washington and Beijing.

